            "costUsd": 0.2775,
            "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
            "answer": "**Fault:** the GNSS lead's UART wires were modelled straight-through instead of crossed at the flight controller pads — the GNSS RX wire landed on the FC's RX1 pad and the GNSS TX wire on TX1, so both ends would talk on one line and listen on the other.\n\n**Fix:** in the `gnss_pads` link in `src/quadcopter.uhd.ts` I swapped the two pads, so the lead's RX wire (p2) now goes to `fc:uart1_tx` and its TX wire (p3) to `fc:uart1_rx`. This matches the lead's own usage note (\"GNSS RX to TX1, GNSS TX to RX1\").\n\n`npx protoboard check` now reports 0 errors. The stale-route warning on the GNSS lead also cleared with the fix. Four warnings remain: three for vendor CAD files the catalog does not distribute for the Matek M9N, and the motor peak current warning against the battery's continuous rating.\n\nThe fix is in the model only: if the physical lead was actually soldered RX-to-RX as the model had it, those two wires need swapping on the board too.",
